    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d22">

                <pb facs="0112" n="26" sameAs="frus:frus1927v01/pg26"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15 a 1/83: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Houghton</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-03-09T16:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 9, 1927—4
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received March 9—2:30
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>59. Department’s No. 49, March 8, 4 p.m. Chamberlain is in Geneva. I saw
                        Tyrrell,<anchor n="22" xml:id="d22-f1"/> who showed me a copy of cable sent
                    yesterday to British Embassy at Washington; he said a copy had been forwarded to
                    Tokyo and would in all probability remove any doubts there in regard to British
                    acceptance.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Houghton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d22-f1" n="22" xml:id="d22-f1-content">
                <p>Sir William George Tyrrell, British Permanent Under Secretary of State for
                    Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
